1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,629
Most pages mix content that rarely changes with content that depends on the visitor.

2
00:00:06,629 --> 00:00:11,758
The Dynamic Cache module stores sections of markup, each with its own rules.

3
00:00:11,758 --> 00:00:17,200
You turn it on, cache blocks and shapes, and remove entries when content changes.

4
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:26,668
A page cache stores the whole response, so one personal detail forces you

5
00:00:26,668 --> 00:00:29,379
to skip it or to vary the entire page.

6
00:00:29,379 --> 00:00:35,480
Dynamic Cache works on sections of markup instead, and each section has its own cache policy.

7
00:00:36,230 --> 00:00:37,892
Sections can nest.

8
00:00:37,892 --> 00:00:43,849
In this layout, section A1 varies by role and section B1 varies by query string.

9
00:00:43,849 --> 00:00:46,620
Each section is stored on its own.

10
00:00:47,370 --> 00:00:52,193
On the first request, every shape is evaluated and the cacheable sections are

11
00:00:52,193 --> 00:00:54,924
stored through the IDynamicCache service.

12
00:00:54,924 --> 00:01:00,386
Later requests read the markup from the cache, and those shapes are not processed again.

13
00:01:00,386 --> 00:01:02,710
Entries stay in memory by default.

14
00:01:06,955 --> 00:01:11,716
The feature is called Dynamic Cache, and it is listed in the Performance

15
00:01:11,716 --> 00:01:13,975
category under Tools, Features.

16
00:01:13,975 --> 00:01:18,065
The Blog recipe enables it, so on this site it is already on.

17
00:01:18,526 --> 00:01:20,613
Cache Mode lists four options.

18
00:01:20,613 --> 00:01:25,888
From environment enables caching in Production only, Enabled turns it on, and Enabled

19
00:01:25,888 --> 00:01:30,293
with cache debug mode also writes comments around each cached section.

20
00:01:30,293 --> 00:01:31,916
Disabled turns it off.

21
00:01:32,367 --> 00:01:37,367
The default is From environment, so a site running in Development stores nothing.

22
00:01:37,367 --> 00:01:39,436
Let's choose Enabled and save.

23
00:01:39,436 --> 00:01:43,287
Saving reloads the tenant, and the mode applies from then on.

24
00:01:47,487 --> 00:01:50,297
The Liquid cache block wraps any markup.

25
00:01:50,297 --> 00:01:54,208
Its first argument is the cache id, the only required one.

26
00:01:54,208 --> 00:01:59,279
The first request stores everything between the cache and end cache tags, and

27
00:01:59,279 --> 00:02:01,357
later requests read it back.

28
00:02:02,107 --> 00:02:05,365
vary_by takes contexts that split the entry:

29
00:02:05,365 --> 00:02:11,557
user, user.roles, query, route and features, where query and features also accept a name.

30
00:02:11,557 --> 00:02:15,207
dependencies takes tags that invalidate the entry.

31
00:02:15,207 --> 00:02:18,857
expires_after and expires_sliding take time spans.

32
00:02:19,607 --> 00:02:23,384
Any other value you give vary_by is used as it is, so a

33
00:02:23,384 --> 00:02:27,099
content item id gives each content item its own entry.

34
00:02:27,099 --> 00:02:31,867
With no expiration, a Liquid block uses a sliding window of one minute.

35
00:02:32,617 --> 00:02:38,566
With cache debug mode enabled, each cached block is wrapped in comments in the page source.

36
00:02:38,566 --> 00:02:43,043
They list its cache id, what it varies by, its dependencies and its

37
00:02:43,043 --> 00:02:46,417
expiration, which helps when an entry misbehaves.

38
00:02:50,587 --> 00:02:53,579
This About page renders through a Liquid template.

39
00:02:53,579 --> 00:02:56,305
The first line shows the time of the request.

40
00:02:56,305 --> 00:03:00,152
The second line sits in a cache block that depends on this content

41
00:03:00,152 --> 00:03:02,397
item and expires after five minutes.

42
00:03:02,862 --> 00:03:04,356
Let's reload the page.

43
00:03:04,356 --> 00:03:08,574
The request time changes, but the cached time stays the same, because the

44
00:03:08,574 --> 00:03:10,442
block is read from the cache.

45
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:15,315
Next, we open Content Items, edit the About page, and click Publish.

46
00:03:15,315 --> 00:03:19,490
Publishing removes the entries that depend on this content item.

47
00:03:19,989 --> 00:03:23,058
Back on the About page, both times have changed.

48
00:03:23,058 --> 00:03:27,549
The block was rendered again, stored again, and serves the next requests.

49
00:03:31,749 --> 00:03:35,999
The Blog Theme caches its main menu with attributes on the shape.

50
00:03:35,999 --> 00:03:42,224
cache_id names the entry, cache_fixed_duration sets five minutes, cache_tag adds a dependency, and

51
00:03:42,224 --> 00:03:45,516
cache_context varies it by the roles of the user.

52
00:03:45,516 --> 00:03:48,689
Razor tags use the same attributes with dashes.

53
00:03:49,439 --> 00:03:54,184
For a block of Razor markup, use the dynamic-cache tag helper.

54
00:03:54,184 --> 00:04:01,094
It takes a cache-id, vary-by, dependencies, and expires-on, expires-after or expires-sliding.

55
00:04:01,094 --> 00:04:06,119
Without an expiration, it uses a sliding window of thirty seconds.

56
00:04:06,869 --> 00:04:11,158
In a display driver, call Cache on the shape result with a cache

57
00:04:11,158 --> 00:04:12,629
id and a callback.

58
00:04:12,629 --> 00:04:17,347
The Notifications module adds the user context, tags and an expiration.

59
00:04:17,347 --> 00:04:20,289
Without the callback, Cache has no effect.

60
00:04:24,489 --> 00:04:26,175
Dependencies are tags.

61
00:04:26,175 --> 00:04:31,654
When a content item is published, unpublished or removed, the Contents module removes

62
00:04:31,654 --> 00:04:33,460
its content item id tag.

63
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:38,699
The Alias module does the same for an alias, and the Autoroute module for a slug.

64
00:04:39,449 --> 00:04:43,609
Your own code calls RemoveTagAsync on ITagCache with any tag.

65
00:04:43,609 --> 00:04:48,452
Every entry stored with that tag is removed, and its section is rendered

66
00:04:48,452 --> 00:04:50,439
again on the next request.

67
00:04:51,189 --> 00:04:53,873
Nested Liquid blocks share what they learn.

68
00:04:53,873 --> 00:04:58,310
When a nested block renders, its dependencies are added to the block around

69
00:04:58,310 --> 00:05:00,611
it, and the shorter expiration wins.

70
00:05:00,611 --> 00:05:03,569
A nested block read from the cache adds nothing.

71
00:05:04,319 --> 00:05:08,771
When the dependencies are not known in advance, such as the items a

72
00:05:08,771 --> 00:05:14,748
query returns, call cache_dependency inside the block, or the cache-dependency tag in Razor.

73
00:05:14,748 --> 00:05:19,749
Here, each item adds its own content item id to the recent blog posts entry.

74
00:05:20,499 --> 00:05:24,648
A cache hit does not run the shape, so scripts and styles registered

75
00:05:24,648 --> 00:05:26,610
while it renders are skipped.

76
00:05:26,610 --> 00:05:31,489
The Notifications module keeps its resources in a separate shape for this reason.

77
00:05:35,689 --> 00:05:39,040
To add a context, implement ICacheContextProvider.

78
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:43,350
It receives the contexts a section asked for and adds entries to a

79
00:05:43,350 --> 00:05:47,001
list, as the route provider does with the request path.

80
00:05:47,001 --> 00:05:52,149
The built-in providers are scoped services, and you register yours the same way.

81
00:05:52,899 --> 00:05:57,321
Entries live in IDynamicCache, which is based on IDistributedCache:

82
00:05:57,321 --> 00:06:00,350
memory by default, or a store such as Redis.

83
00:06:00,350 --> 00:06:04,530
If a read or write fails, the cache is skipped for the failover

84
00:06:04,530 --> 00:06:07,378
retry latency, thirty seconds by default.

85
00:06:07,378 --> 00:06:09,559
A separate video covers Redis.

86
00:06:11,559 --> 00:06:16,588
Dynamic Cache stores sections of markup, varies them by context, and removes them

87
00:06:16,588 --> 00:06:17,976
by tag or by time.

88
00:06:17,976 --> 00:06:19,479
Thanks for watching.

